course: dictionary
lesson: 6729
title: One small word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: OK is one of the commonest words in English, and it can do several jobs. Read the words around it to find out which job it is doing. «Is it OK if I sit here?» asks whether something is allowed, so OK means acceptable.
explain: «Are you OK?» is different. Here OK means well, not ill or hurt. This is a kind question, and if someone is not OK, tell a trusted adult.
explain: On its own, OK can mean "yes, I agree", as in «OK, let's go!». Mum can also OK something, as in «Mum OK'd the trip», which is informal and means she gave permission. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then try your meaning in it to check.
fact: OK can be an adjective, an adverb, an exclamation or a verb. Its meaning depends on the sentence around it.
fact: «It's OK to feel sad» means feeling sad is acceptable. It is not something you are doing wrong.
fact: «It's going to be OK» means things will turn out well enough.
fact: «Teasing someone about their size is never OK» shows that OK means acceptable, and that this kind of teasing is not allowed.
fact: «OK» was first printed in America in 1839, so a knight or a Roman saying it in a film is out of time.
exercise: «Is it OK if I sit here?» What does OK mean in this sentence? | Very loud | *Acceptable, or allowed | Well and not hurt | Yes, I agree
exercise: «Are you OK?» What does OK mean in this question? | Yes, I agree | Tidy and neat | Allowed to go | *Not ill or hurt
exercise: «Mum OK'd the trip.» What does OK'd mean? | *Gave permission | Ran out of time | Paid for lunch | Cancelled
test: In «OK, let's go!», what does OK mean? | Not ill or hurt | Very old | Acceptable, or allowed | *Yes, I agree
test: «It's going to be OK.» What does OK mean here? | Given permission | Ill and in bed | *Things will turn out well enough | Very loud and cheerful
exam: Why is «Are you OK?» an important question to ask? | *It is a kind question, and if someone is not OK you should tell a trusted adult | It means the same as «Yes, I agree» | Because it helps you win games | Because it is a grammar rule
